<Layouts.app flash={@flash}>
  <article id="about-page" class="lbm-about">
    <header class="lbm-hero lbm-about-hero">
      <p class="lbm-eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1 class="lbm-hero-title">The math that holds things up</h1>
      <p class="lbm-hero-lede">
        Load Bearing Math is a collection of short, hands-on walkthroughs of the math behind the
        systems we use every day: search, rate limiters, autoscalers, capacity planning, and more.
        Every piece is worked out by an engineer who uses this math on the job and has spent years
        teaching it.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section id="about-mission" class="lbm-about-section">
      <p>
        So much of computer science, and of the technology around us, is built on mathematics. As
        software matured, a lot of that math got abstracted away, and the same thing is happening again
        with AI. That's fine until something breaks. Then the math is the only thing that tells you why.
      </p>
      <p>
        It's also more approachable than its reputation. Most math aversion isn't about ability. It's
        the overwhelming feeling of looking at a problem all at once. That feeling fades as the problem
        gets broken into smaller pieces, and once things start clicking, it's one of the most enjoyable
        feelings there is. It took until calculus for all of it to click for me, so if it hasn't clicked
        for you yet, you're in good company.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="about-who" class="lbm-about-section" aria-labelledby="about-who-heading">
      <h2 id="about-who-heading">Who's behind it</h2>
      <p>
        I'm Andrew Ferguson, a software engineer with a focus on systems. I have a B.S. in Computer
        Science with a minor in Mathematics, and since graduating I've worked in aerospace, space
        systems, and large-scale systems integration. Nearly all of it runs on the kind of math you'll
        find on this site.
      </p>
      <p>
        Teaching has always run alongside the engineering. Understanding something well enough to
        explain it is half the fun, and I'm glad to pass along what I've learned.
      </p>
      <p>
        I take on select consulting, advisory, and full-time engineering work, and I still teach
        one-on-one.
        <.link navigate={~p"/work-with-me"} id="about-work-link">
          Work with me <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </.link>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section
      id="about-teaching"
      class="lbm-about-section"
      aria-labelledby="about-teaching-heading"
    >
      <h2 id="about-teaching-heading">How I work</h2>
      <p>
        Whether it's a system or a student, the approach is the same: break the problem into pieces
        small enough to reason about, work them by hand, and check the answer against reality. "You'll
        never use this" is one of the most common things said about math, and it's rarely true:
        <.link navigate={~p"/cosine-similarity"}>cosine similarity</.link>
        is how search engines decide what counts as similar, and
        <.link navigate={~p"/littles-law"}>Little's Law</.link>
        is how you size a server's worker pool.
      </p>
      <p>
        With students, that means they work through the problems themselves, with me as a guide, and we
        come back to the ideas later so they stick. With systems, it means numbers before opinions:
        measure it, model it, then change it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="about-site" class="lbm-about-section" aria-labelledby="about-site-heading">
      <h2 id="about-site-heading">About the topics</h2>
      <p>
        Every topic here is worked out by hand. The worked examples sit behind a button so you can skip
        them, or click through and check the work. If you find a mistake, please tell me. And if there's
        something you'd like to see covered, use the Suggest a topic button at the top of the page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="about-personal" class="lbm-about-section">
      <p class="lbm-about-personal">
        Outside of math, I'm based in New Hampshire and have lived all over New England. I like
        skiing, gaming, reading, and spending time with my dog, cat, and newborn.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="lbm-landing-hero-actions">
      <.button navigate={~p"/topics"} variant="primary" id="about-topics">
        Browse all topics <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
      </.button>
      <.button navigate={~p"/services"} id="about-services">Work with me</.button>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layouts.app>
